@attribute [Route("/components/" + nameof(HxDialog))]

<ApiDoc Type="typeof(HxDialog)">

    <DocHeading Title="Basic usage" />
	<Demo Type="typeof(HxDialog_Demo)" Tabs="false" />

    <DocHeading Title="Footer" />
	<p>
		Use the <code>FooterTemplate</code> to add action buttons or other content to the bottom of the dialog.
		The footer renders as a Bootstrap <code>dialog-footer</code>, so multiple actions are spaced and aligned to the end automatically.
	</p>
	<Demo Type="typeof(HxDialog_Demo_Footer)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Static backdrop" />
	<p>When the backdrop is set to <code>DialogBackdrop.Static</code>, the dialog will not close when clicking outside of it.</p>
	<Demo Type="typeof(HxDialog_Demo_Backdrop)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Scrolling long content" />
	<p>
		The page behind a modal dialog does not scroll. When a dialog becomes too long for the user's viewport or device, the whole dialog scrolls
		(the header and footer scroll with the content). Shorter dialogs do not clip their content, so dropdowns, autosuggests or date pickers can overflow the dialog.
		Try the demo below to see what we mean.
	</p>
	<Demo Type="typeof(HxDialog_Demo_Scrolling)" />

	<p>
		You can also create a scrollable dialog by setting the <code>Scrollable</code> parameter: the dialog always fits the viewport,
		only the dialog body scrolls and the header and footer stay visible. Note that the body clips its content, which affects inline dropdowns near its edges.
	</p>
	<Demo Type="typeof(HxDialog_Demo_Scrollable)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Vertical centering" />
	<p>Dialogs are vertically centered natively, there is no parameter to set. The dialog is built on the native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> element, which the browser centers in the viewport by default.</p>

    <DocHeading Title="Animation" />
	<p>Use the <code>Animation</code> parameter to choose the open/close animation of the dialog. The default is <code>DialogAnimation.Fade</code>, use <code>DialogAnimation.None</code> to disable the animation.</p>
	<Demo Type="typeof(HxDialog_Demo_Animation)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Stacked dialogs" />
	<p>
		Dialogs stack on top of each other (the browser keeps open modal dialogs in the top layer). When you call <code>ShowAsync()</code> on a dialog while another one is open,
		the new dialog opens above it and the dialog below stays open (and inert) until the top one closes. This is how a confirmation from <code>IHxMessageBoxService</code> works inside a dialog.
	</p>
	<Demo Type="typeof(HxDialog_Demo_Stacking)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Swapping dialogs" />
	<p>To <strong>swap</strong> dialogs instead, close the current dialog before opening the next one (<code>HideAsync()</code> followed by <code>ShowAsync()</code>) &ndash; the first one fades out as the second one opens, and the backdrop stays visible the whole time without flickering.</p>
	<Demo Type="typeof(HxDialog_Demo_Swapping)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Non-modal dialogs" />
	<p>Set <code>NonModal="true"</code> to open the dialog with the browser-native <code>show()</code> instead of <code>showModal()</code>. A non-modal dialog has no backdrop, no focus trap, and does not block the rest of the page.</p>
	<Demo Type="typeof(HxDialog_Demo_NonModal)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Optional sizes" />
	<Demo Type="typeof(HxDialog_Demo_Size)" />

    <DocHeading Title="Fullscreen dialog" />
	<Demo Type="typeof(HxDialog_Demo_Fullscreen)" />
</ApiDoc>